body:
{
  "pains": [
    {
      "id": "pain:bill-outruns-the-infra",
      "statement": "Our monitoring bill is growing faster than the infrastructure it watches, and we find out at the end of the month.",
      "element": "b2b:functional:cost_reduction",
      "shape": {
        "surface": "The invoice lands bigger than last month and nobody deliberately changed the product: sidecar containers, crash-loop restarts and application logs each turn out to be billed as their own unit.",
        "operational": "Engineers re-architect around the pricing model instead of around the workload - fewer and larger instances, no sidecar proxy, application logs filtered or dropped - and spend engineering time on the invoice rather than on the product.",
        "personal": null,
        "personal_note": "No source read shows what the bill costs the person who chose the vendor. The Hacker News cost threads stop at the architecture change and at the sales conversation ('unless your a whale they give zero fucks'), and the G2 review stops at 'It's very costly'. Looked in the HN Datadog-cost threads, the Datadog review pages syndicated on AWS Marketplace, and the on-call threads; the people who own the budget mostly do not post in public. Asked as q:pains-personal-cost."
      },
      "workflow": "The month-end comparison of the observability invoice against the AWS bill, and what it forces back upstream: the step in Terraform where instance sizes are chosen to fit per-host billing rather than the workload, the decision not to run an Istio/Linkerd sidecar because each sidecar bills as a host, and the log pipeline where application logs get filtered before they are shipped.",
      "type": "felt",
      "confidence": "high",
      "triggers": [
        {
          "event": "Scaling infrastructure elastically: the cost model that rewards scaling AWS down punishes you on the monitoring bill, so the saving reverses.",
          "cites": ["src:1"]
        },
        {
          "event": "Adopting a service mesh, where every sidecar container is billed as an additional host.",
          "cites": ["src:2"]
        },
        {
          "event": "Turning on application log shipping, where volume is driven by traffic rather than by headcount or host count.",
          "cites": ["src:3", "src:23"]
        }
      ],
      "who_feels": ["economic_buyer", "end_user"],
      "by_role": {
        "end_user": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "DataDog have an extractive pricing model.",
              "cites": ["src:1"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "one personal case I can attest to was case the changes would halve the AWS bill but increase the DataDog bill by more than 10 times",
              "cites": ["src:1"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "datadog apparently bills sidecar containers as additional hosts so using sidecar proxy would have doubled our datadog bill",
              "cites": ["src:2"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "It's very costly. Sending application logs to Datadog can lead to unpredictable costs.",
              "cites": ["src:3"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "collecting all our events would double our infrastructure budget",
              "cites": ["src:23"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "Engineering time spent shaping infrastructure around someone else's price list: instance sizing, abandoning a mesh rollout, and filtering logs they would rather keep.",
          "fears": "That the next architectural decision they make - a mesh, an autoscaler, a new log stream - silently multiplies a bill they do not control and cannot see until the period closes.",
          "dream_outcome": "Telemetry priced on what they actually send, so the right architecture and the cheap bill are the same decision."
        },
        "economic_buyer": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "I was the cofounder of a small SaaS that was blowing >$500k / year on our cloud stack",
              "cites": ["src:4"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_looking",
              "verbatim": "Then you'll want something like datadog because someone is reporting somewhere that a page is taking 10 seconds to load, but you can't replicate it. +$300 / m cloud service.",
              "cites": ["src:4"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "A line item that compounds with every new service, on a stack where the same buyer is already paying for error tracking, dashboards and a warehouse.",
          "fears": "That observability quietly becomes one of the largest line items in the infrastructure budget with no ceiling and no obvious moment to stop it.",
          "dream_outcome": "One predictable number for observability that they can defend in a budget review without asking engineers to spend a sprint on it."
        }
      },
      "segments": ["seg:platform", "seg:startup"],
      "feature_ref": "feat:logs",
      "feature_note": "feat:logs (Loki) and feat:metrics (Mimir) are the closest answers, because the complaint is loudest about log volume and about per-host pricing on metrics. But the thing buyers actually describe is the shape of the price list, not a feature: no row in brain/company names usage-based pricing or the absence of a per-host fee, which is what would kill this pain.",
      "cites": ["src:1", "src:2", "src:3", "src:4", "src:23"]
    },
    {
      "id": "pain:paged-for-nothing",
      "statement": "I get woken at 2am by a page that has already fixed itself, and the next day I'm useless and somehow it's my attitude that's the problem.",
      "element": "b2b:individual:reduced_anxiety",
      "shape": {
        "surface": "The phone goes off in the night for an alert that auto-resolves, or for a monitor that fired because the monitoring vendor itself was down rather than production.",
        "operational": "The team mutes or hand-tunes noisy monitors, keeps a runbook step for checking whether the monitoring provider is down, learns to ignore the alert that comes back every week, and burns on-call shifts triaging instead of building.",
        "personal": "The blame lands on the person rather than on the alert: one engineer was reprimanded and nearly fired for being angry about a junk page, and another describes the guilt of sleeping through an escalation as incredibly stressful."
      },
      "workflow": "The on-call rotation between the alerting rules (Prometheus/Alertmanager, or the vendor's monitors) and the phone at 2am: the step where the page fires, the responder opens the dashboard, finds it already resolved or finds that the monitoring vendor is the outage, and goes back to bed - and the shift handover the next morning where the same alert is passed on rather than fixed.",
      "type": "felt",
      "confidence": "high",
      "triggers": [
        {
          "event": "An outage at the monitoring provider itself, which turns every monitor into a false page at once.",
          "cites": ["src:5"]
        },
        {
          "event": "A recurring alert the team has learned to ignore landing on someone else's shift, where it is not yet furniture.",
          "cites": ["src:8"]
        }
      ],
      "who_feels": ["end_user", "champion"],
      "by_role": {
        "end_user": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "I got reprimanded and almost fired recently for being pissed at someone who woke me up for some bs page.",
              "cites": ["src:6"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "Waking up an oncall dev at 2am saying all of production is down when it is just datadog is bad for morale.",
              "cites": ["src:5"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "But the guilt I felt was… incredibly stressful.",
              "cites": ["src:7"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "I don't like false alerts. I don't like paging folks about false alerts.",
              "cites": ["src:5"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "Sleep, and the day after the night: interrupted nights for alerts that resolve themselves, plus the standing risk of being judged on how they reacted to being woken.",
          "fears": "That normalising the noise is what finally makes them miss the page that mattered - and that the miss, not the noise, is what gets remembered.",
          "dream_outcome": "A pager that only goes off when a human has to do something, and a night off when it doesn't."
        },
        "champion": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. It's the single biggest quality of life thing that you can do to help with the annoyance that is on call.",
              "cites": ["src:8"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "It becomes then a game of normalizing deviance and burnout: \"oh, we just ignored that one last time\". Ok, why are they alerts then if they can be ignored? It's just going to murder people's spirit after a while.",
              "cites": ["src:8"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "to_their_boss",
              "verbatim": "This is the time where your mettle as a manager is really tested against your higher-ups. If your people are putting in unscheduled time, you better be ready to cough up something in return.",
              "cites": ["src:8"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "Feature work that does not ship because the on-call engineer spent the shift on alerts, plus recovery days owed after bad nights, plus the argument upstairs to pay for them.",
          "fears": "Attrition: that the people who carry the rota quietly decide the job is not worth it, and that they are the manager who let the noise do it.",
          "dream_outcome": "An on-call shift boring enough that being on it is just another week, and a number they can show their own boss to prove the noise is falling."
        }
      },
      "segments": ["seg:platform"],
      "feature_ref": "feat:irm",
      "feature_note": "feat:irm owns the schedule, the routing and the incident, which is the machinery around the page. Nothing in brain/company promises fewer false pages, and that is what every quote here is about. feat:ai is the nearest claim and it is about finding the cause once you are awake, not about deciding whether to wake you.",
      "cites": ["src:5", "src:6", "src:7", "src:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "pain:dashboards-nobody-trusts",
      "statement": "We have more dashboards than anyone can watch, half of them are broken or duplicated, and in an incident I can't tell you which one is right.",
      "element": "b2b:ease_of_doing_business:organization",
      "shape": {
        "surface": "Wall screens and dashboard folders full of panels that are stale, broken, or built once for a postmortem and never opened again.",
        "operational": "People fall back on the two or three panels they personally trust and build a fresh dashboard per incident rather than finding the existing one; metrics get collected that no query reads, and queries exist that nothing runs, with no way to spot either.",
        "personal": "The person carries the map in their head - 'There are only so many dashboards I can watch' - so knowing which panel is true belongs to whoever has been on the team longest, and is on them at 3am."
      },
      "workflow": "The incident channel at the moment someone asks which dashboard shows this: the dashboard list in Grafana or Datadog, the team's wall screen, and the runbook that names the alert but does not link the panel - the step where the responder opens three dashboards and trusts none of them. It also bites after a backend migration, where panels point at a data source that no longer exists.",
      "type": "felt",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "triggers": [
        {
          "event": "A telemetry backend or data-source migration, which leaves panels and queries pointing at something that is gone and exposes which code was never covered.",
          "cites": ["src:10"]
        },
        {
          "event": "A postmortem action item that adds one more dashboard nobody puts in a runbook.",
          "cites": ["src:10"]
        }
      ],
      "who_feels": ["end_user"],
      "by_role": {
        "end_user": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "Most of the time they were badly maintained and broken, so only the _really_ key metrics worked.",
              "cites": ["src:9"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "Great for finding out what went wrong, terrible at alerting when it went wrong.",
              "cites": ["src:9"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "Meanwhile we found a bunch of stats that nobody looks at, meaning the SNR goes down over time. There are only so many dashboards I can watch.",
              "cites": ["src:10"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_looking",
              "verbatim": "queries that exist but never run (that dashboard nobody remembers making and isn't in the runbooks anywhere) would be a great goal for a next level of maturity",
              "cites": ["src:10"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "Minutes at the top of every incident spent deciding what to believe, and the standing chore of maintaining panels that turn out to be watched by nobody.",
          "fears": "That the dashboard everyone is staring at during an outage is the stale one, and the decision made from it is wrong.",
          "dream_outcome": "To know which dashboards and queries are actually used, retire the rest, and land on the one true panel from the alert itself."
        }
      },
      "segments": ["seg:enterprise", "seg:platform"],
      "feature_ref": "feat:dashboards",
      "feature_note": "feat:dashboards is what makes them; nothing in brain/company finds the stale ones, tells you which panel a given alert should open, or reports which queries no human has run. Both quotes ask for exactly that, and the gap is a finding for the offer step.",
      "cites": ["src:9", "src:10"]
    },
    {
      "id": "pain:stack-never-stays-still",
      "statement": "Every year something in the monitoring stack is deprecated or re-architected and I'm back explaining to my boss why we're rebuilding monitoring again.",
      "element": "b2b:ease_of_doing_business:stability",
      "shape": {
        "surface": "Components get deprecated on a two-to-three year cycle, a framework change breaks existing dashboards, the default Helm template renders to thousands of lines, and a major version of the metrics store suddenly wants Apache Kafka alongside it.",
        "operational": "The platform team re-plans monitoring on a cadence nobody asked for: migrating agents across clusters, re-templating chart values, re-testing dashboards after a migration, and standing up new infrastructure underneath the storage layer. For teams doing it from scratch it means assembling Prometheus, Loki and Grafana and wiring auth and dashboards by hand before any of it watches anything.",
        "personal": "It falls on the engineer who recommended the stack to go back to their boss each year and justify the rebuild - which is the thing they say out loud they want to stop doing."
      },
      "workflow": "The upgrade of the self-hosted stack: the kube-prometheus-stack or Grafana Helm values file, the agent rollout across clusters, and the step in the release notes where a component is marked deprecated and the dashboards have to be migrated. For a new cluster it is the day-one step after kubeadm, installing Prometheus, Loki and Grafana and configuring auth before anything is observable.",
      "type": "felt",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "triggers": [
        {
          "event": "A major version that changes the architecture rather than the features - Mimir 3.0 requiring Apache Kafka is the example buyers name.",
          "cites": ["src:11"]
        },
        {
          "event": "Standing up a new Kubernetes cluster, where the whole monitoring stack has to be assembled from separate projects again.",
          "cites": ["src:12"]
        },
        {
          "event": "A deprecation notice for a component the team already runs in production, such as the on-call or agent products.",
          "cites": ["src:11"]
        }
      ],
      "who_feels": ["champion", "end_user"],
      "by_role": {
        "champion": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "to_their_boss",
              "verbatim": "I can finally stop explaining to my boss why we need to re-structure the monitoring stack every year",
              "cites": ["src:11"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "I want stability for my monitoring; I want it boring",
              "cites": ["src:11"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "Yes, Mimir in version 3.0 needs Apache Kafka to work.",
              "cites": ["src:11"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_refusing",
              "verbatim": "all this together makes me uncomfortable recommending Grafana stuff",
              "cites": ["src:11"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "A recurring migration project they have to fund and defend, on a system that is supposed to be infrastructure rather than a product they own.",
          "fears": "That they are the person who bet the org's monitoring on a stack that will move again next year, and that the next move needs a Kafka cluster they now also have to run.",
          "dream_outcome": "Monitoring that is boring: they set it up, it keeps working through upgrades, and it never appears on the roadmap again."
        },
        "end_user": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "You need to spend a lot of time, intalling prometheus, loki, grafana, configure dashboards, configure every piece of software. Those are very different softwares from different vendors. It's a mess.",
              "cites": ["src:12"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_looking",
              "verbatim": "I just get annoyed having to host Grafana and setting it up, the database up, configuring auth, etc.",
              "cites": ["src:13"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_comparing",
              "verbatim": "In my mind I would prefer to have things like Prometheus, Logs, and Metrics stored on prem mainly due to the volume of logs and metrics we create.",
              "cites": ["src:13"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "Days of setup and re-setup that produce no product: charts, auth, databases and dashboards wired by hand for each cluster.",
          "fears": "That the assembly work never ends, and that keeping the monitoring alive becomes its own permanent job.",
          "dream_outcome": "Somebody else runs the front end and the plumbing while the data they care about stays where they want it."
        }
      },
      "segments": ["seg:platform", "seg:startup"],
      "feature_ref": null,
      "feature_note": "No feature in brain/company kills this, and it is the uncomfortable one: the churn these buyers name is Grafana's own release and deprecation cadence, so the answer is a support and deprecation commitment, not a feature. Grafana Cloud as a product does remove the hosting half - chosenken is asking for precisely the split it offers - but no feature row in the company artifact names managed operation, so there is nothing to point at.",
      "cites": ["src:11", "src:12", "src:13"]
    },
    {
      "id": "pain:free-stack-hits-a-wall",
      "statement": "The open-source stack was fine until one label or one more hundred hosts, and then queries crawl and the whole thing falls over.",
      "element": "b2b:functional:scalability",
      "shape": {
        "surface": "Queries that used to return instantly become unmanageable and slow, and a per-request or per-customer label takes the metrics system down with it.",
        "operational": "Teams swap the storage layer out from under Prometheus, tune retention aggressively, throw bigger hardware at a single node, or build a parallel cluster and migrate onto it.",
        "personal": null,
        "personal_note": "No source read shows what the ceiling costs the person. The three comments are asides inside threads about time-series databases and log storage; each describes the system failing and none describes the night, the review or the argument that followed. Looked in the Hacker News cardinality and time-series threads; Reddit, where this is most often told as a war story, would not fetch. Asked as q:pains-scale-wall."
      },
      "workflow": "The self-hosted Prometheus and Grafana stack as host count and label cardinality grow: the step where a per-request or per-customer label is added to a metric, active series jump, and the dashboard query behind an alert starts timing out - and the capacity conversation at roughly a hundred hosts, where the stock single-node setup has no sharding or HA to fall back on.",
      "type": "felt",
      "confidence": "low",
      "triggers": [
        {
          "event": "Adding a high-cardinality label such as per-request or per-customer metrics.",
          "cites": ["src:16"]
        },
        {
          "event": "Passing roughly a hundred hosts and applications on the stock single-node open-source stack.",
          "cites": ["src:14"]
        }
      ],
      "who_feels": ["end_user"],
      "by_role": {
        "end_user": {
          "says": [
            {
              "situation": "when_it_bites",
              "verbatim": "the cardinality is too high and it blows up the whole system",
              "cites": ["src:16"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "venting",
              "verbatim": "Both these open source tools are entirely single node. There is no form of sharding nor high availability nor horizontal scaling.",
              "cites": ["src:14"]
            },
            {
              "situation": "when_comparing",
              "verbatim": "You get high enough (hitting a metric with >100k unique labels), queries become unmanageable and incredibly slow when backed by the stock datastore (tsdb). However there are backing datastores (TimescaleDB, InfluxDB, VictoriaMetrics, etc) that ingest Prometheus metrics and enable higher cardinality.",
              "cites": ["src:15"]
            }
          ],
          "costs_them": "A rebuild at the worst moment: the ceiling is discovered while the system is already under load, and the fix is a storage migration rather than a config change.",
          "fears": "That the metric they most need during an incident is exactly the high-cardinality one they were told not to add.",
          "dream_outcome": "Adding the label they want without thinking about active series, and keeping the query language and dashboards they already have."
        }
      },
      "segments": ["seg:platform", "seg:startup"],
      "feature_ref": "feat:metrics",
      "feature_note": "feat:metrics (Mimir) is the direct answer to the cardinality and single-node ceiling, and feat:logs is the same answer for log retention. Worth flagging for the positioning step: the same population that wants this also names Mimir's own operating cost in pain:stack-never-stays-still, so the answer to one pain is an instance of the other.",
      "cites": ["src:14", "src:15", "src:16"]
    }
  ],
  "awareness": {
    "default": {
      "level": "solution",
      "rationale": "Across every segment swept, buyers do not ask whether tools exist - they name three or four by name and weigh them. One comment maps each paid service to its self-hosted replacement ('Datadog -> Self Hosted Prometheus / Grafana'); another answers a Prometheus ceiling by listing TimescaleDB, InfluxDB and VictoriaMetrics as alternative backing stores. That is solution-aware, not problem-aware.",
      "cites": ["src:4", "src:15", "src:14"]
    },
    "seg:platform": {
      "level": "product",
      "rationale": "Platform and SRE buyers know Grafana specifically, already run it, and are making a decision about it rather than discovering it: one writes a whole post about why he can no longer recommend Grafana after years of running it, and another knows Grafana Cloud exists and is asking whether he can buy the hosted front end while keeping Prometheus and Loki on prem. Both are past 'solutions exist'.",
      "cites": ["src:11", "src:13"]
    }
  },
  "stats": [
    {
      "id": "stat:tools-per-org",
      "stat": "Organizations run about 4.4 observability tools on average, and 73% still lack full-stack observability.",
      "scope": "New Relic's 2025 Observability Forecast, a survey of 1,700 IT and engineering leaders globally, as reported by Network World. Grafana Labs' own 2025 Observability Survey (1,255 responses collected 18 Sept 2024 to 2 Jan 2025) counts an average of 16 data sources configured in Grafana, which is a different and much larger measure of the same sprawl; the lower and independently-run number is the one used here. Both are self-reported surveys, not measured inventories.",
      "cites": ["src:17", "src:18"]
    },
    {
      "id": "stat:cutting-data-to-afford-it",
      "stat": "Nearly 80% of teams filter, archive or offload logs to control cost.",
      "scope": "Imply's 'The Breaking Point for Observability Leaders', reported by APMdigest in April 2026; sample size not published. The 2024 Logz.io Observability Pulse Survey puts a broader version of the same behaviour at 91% actively looking for ways to reduce observability costs; sample size also not published. The narrower 80% is used because it names a specific action rather than an intention. Both numbers trace to vendor-run research, which makes this the weakest number in the map - quote it with the source named, and see q:pains-stat-check.",
      "cites": ["src:19", "src:20"]
    }
  ],
  "keywords": [
    "datadog bill too high",
    "reduce datadog costs",
    "datadog per host pricing sidecar",
    "datadog alternative self hosted",
    "unpredictable log costs",
    "prometheus high cardinality blows up",
    "prometheus single node scaling limit",
    "prometheus 100k unique labels slow queries",
    "self hosted prometheus loki grafana setup",
    "mimir 3.0 kafka requirement",
    "grafana agent deprecated migration",
    "grafana alternative boring monitoring",
    "alert fatigue on call",
    "false alerts waking up on call",
    "too many dashboards nobody looks at",
    "which dashboard during an incident"
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q:pains-first-call",
      "question": "On the first call with your last ten customers, which of these five did they say first, in their own words - the bill, the pager, the dashboards nobody trusts, the stack that keeps moving, or the scale ceiling?",
      "why": "The map is built entirely from strangers in public. Which pain leads a real first call is the one thing that reorders it, and everything downstream reads the order.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence, and the ordering of pains[]"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-personal-cost",
      "question": "When an observability bill blows up, what happens to the person who chose the vendor - who has to explain it, and what do they say?",
      "why": "pain:bill-outruns-the-infra has a null personal rung. The public record stops at the architecture change and the sales conversation; the personal rung is the one that sells, and only you hear it.",
      "would_change": "pains[].shape.personal on pain:bill-outruns-the-infra"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-churn",
      "question": "What did customers who left or downgraded say on the way out - and did any of them name deprecations, forced migrations or a major version that needed new infrastructure?",
      "why": "pain:stack-never-stays-still is a pain about Grafana, evidenced by a practitioner blog and two comments. If churned customers say it too, it stops being a marketing problem and becomes a product commitment; if they never say it, its confidence should drop.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence and pains[].feature_note on pain:stack-never-stays-still"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-scale-wall",
      "question": "For customers who arrived from self-hosted Prometheus, what actually broke - cardinality, retention, query latency, HA - and in what words did they describe it when they first called?",
      "why": "pain:free-stack-hits-a-wall is marked low: both sources exist but are asides in threads about something else, and two are from 2016 and 2020. One customer sentence settles whether this is a current pain or an old one.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence and pains[].shape.personal on pain:free-stack-hits-a-wall"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-unclaimed-features",
      "question": "feat:traces, feat:k6 and feat:ai have no pain pointing at them in this map. Do customers name the problem each one solves, unprompted, and what do they call it?",
      "why": "A feature with no pain beside it either solves something buyers do not say out loud in public, or solves something they do not feel. The positioning and offer steps need to know which, and only your calls can tell them apart.",
      "would_change": "pains[] (a new row per confirmed pain) and pains[].feature_ref"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-fragmentation",
      "question": "Do your customers complain that they cannot get at another team's data - that the tool holds everything but each group uses it differently, so cross-team debugging stalls?",
      "why": "Found once and not twice: a Datadog reviewer says it is hard to dig into logs from other services outside her group because everyone uses it differently, and one engineering team describes jumping from metrics in one vendor into logs in their own. One source each is an anecdote, so it is not a row in the map.",
