  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "src:1",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=38093281",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by techdragon on Datadog's pricing model",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/38093281) because the item page itself returned HTTP 429 on fetch; the API is Hacker News' own search index and returns the stored comment text.",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2023-11-01",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "As a long term DataDog customer… no it's beyond that.\n\nDataDog have an extractive pricing model. It's undergone a number of changes over the years that separated out features previously under the host price into their own products with separate monthly costs. They put limits on the numbers of unique containers you can have per host before charging container fees, so better hope you don't have too many crash-restart loops or. Theres the per unique host per billing period pricing model that makes it impossible to scale your own infrastructure up and down to save on hardware costs, one personal case I can attest to was case the changes would halve the AWS bill but increase the DataDog bill by more than 10 times, a thousand percent… and you would think surely you can talk to the sales team if your use case fits extremely poorly with the pricing model…. Hahaha no… unless your a whale they give zero fucks about giving you any flexibility on price."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:2",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45438010",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by pm90 on service mesh sidecars and Datadog billing",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/45438010).",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-10-01",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I haven't been keeping up but is there still hype over full mesh like istio/linkerd? Ive seen it tried in a couple of places but didn't work super well; the last place couldn't because datadog apparently bills sidecar containers as additional hosts so using sidecar proxy would have doubled our datadog bill."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:3",
      "url": "https://aws.amazon.com/marketplace/reviews/reviews-list/prodview-ba2nz3vsyg46a?page=41",
      "title": "Datadog Agent reviews, AWS Marketplace review list (reviews syndicated from G2), page 41",
      "venue": "AWS Marketplace review list. The reviews on this page are syndicated from G2 and carry G2's question prompts; g2.com and trustradius.com both refused a direct fetch, this page did not.",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2026-09",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Anshuman S., Sep 10, 2026 (G2). What do you dislike about the product? \"It's very costly. Sending application logs to Datadog can lead to unpredictable costs. Providing add-on features is a very complex task. It's difficult to learn for new users.\" What problems is the product solving and how is that benefiting you? \"Despite the cost, I love this tool. It helps in identifying real-time issues very quickly.\"\n\nAmanda C., Jun 10, 2026 (G2). What do you dislike about the product? \"It can be hard to dig into logs from other services outside my group because everyone uses it differently.\" What do you like best? \"I like how integrated Datadog is with other systems, allowing me to see data across the whole organization.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "src:4",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45529822",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by czhu12 on the cost of the cloud services stack, on 'Why Self-Host?'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. The commenter is a founder of his own product (canine.sh) but speaks here as the buyer of the stack he is describing, not as a seller of observability.",
      "speaker": "buyer",
      "published": "2025-10-09",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "This is why I built https://canine.sh -- to make installing all that stuff a single step. I was the cofounder of a small SaaS that was blowing >$500k / year on our cloud stack\n\nWithin the first few weeks, you'll realize you also need sentry, otherwise, errors in production just become digging through logs. Thats a +$40 / m cloud service.\n\nThen you'll want something like datadog because someone is reporting somewhere that a page is taking 10 seconds to load, but you can't replicate it. +$300 / m cloud service.\n\nThen, if you ever want to aggregate data into a dashboard to present to customers -- Looker / Tableau / Omni +$20k / year.\n\nData warehouse + replication? +$150k / year\n\nThis goes on and on and on. The holy grail is to be able to run ALL of these external services in your own infrastructure on a common platform with some level of maintainability.\n\nCloud Sentry -> Self Hosted Sentry\n\nDatadog -> Self Hosted Prometheus / Grafana\n\nLooker -> Self Hosted Metabase\n\nSnowflake -> Self Hosted Clickhouse\n\nETL -> Self Hosted Airbyte"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:5",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=35073050",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by palijer on false pages, on 'DataDog is having a major outage across almost all services'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/35073050).",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2023-03-08",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "If you just want notifications for when datadog is down, their StatusPage does a fine job of clearly communicating incidents.\n\nI wouldn't want to rely on a \"when multiple of our 'missing business metric' monitors alert, check and see if datadog is down\" step in a runbook. I don't like false alerts. I don't like paging folks about false alerts. Waking up an oncall dev at 2am saying all of production is down when it is just datadog is bad for morale. Alert fatigue is a real and measurable issue with consequences. Avoiding false alerts is good. If the notification says \"all of production is down\" and that isn't the case, there is impact for that. I'd much prefer having a StatusPage alert at a lower severity and communication level say \"datadog ingestion is down\".\n\nWe prioritize Time To Communicate as a metric. When we notice and issue in production, we want customers to find out from us that we are investigating instead of troubleshooting and encountering the issue themselves, getting mad, and clogging up our support resources. Flaky alerts here don't work at all for us."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:6",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=32174459",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by tayo42 on being paged, on 'Being on call sucks'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2022-07-21",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I got reprimanded and almost fired recently for being pissed at someone who woke me up for some bs page. Like of course ill be angry, i got woken up for work for no real reason. Now im extra pissed about the managers coming down on my for this instead of correcting the oncall..."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:7",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=32175621",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by pm90 on missing a page, on 'Being on call sucks'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/32175621).",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2022-07-21",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "+1. I am a deep sleeper and Ive missed pages at times that got escalated to the secondary. Nothing major, just something that autoresolved. But the guilt I felt was… incredibly stressful. Because I felt I was letting down people I was familiar with."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:8",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41654011",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by theideaofcoffee on managing on-call, on 'How to manage oncall as an engineering manager?'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/41654011). The commenter is answering as an engineering manager, which is why he is read as champion rather than end user.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-09-26",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. Alert fatigue. It's the single biggest quality of life thing that you can do to help with the annoyance that is on call. If you know you're in store for the same alert again and again, or perhaps even know that you know you're going to get paged, it's hard to think about anything else. It becomes then a game of normalizing deviance and burnout: \"oh, we just ignored that one last time\". Ok, why are they alerts then if they can be ignored? It's just going to murder people's spirit after a while.\n\nSomeone gets called in the middle of the night? Let them take the morning to recover, no questions asked, better yet, the entire day if it was a particularly hairy issue. This is the time where your mettle as a manager is really tested against your higher-ups. If your people are putting in unscheduled time, you better be ready to cough up something in return.\n\nFigure out what's commonly coming up and root cause those issues so they can finally be put to bed (and your on-call can go back to bed, hah).\n\nBeyond that, if someone is on call, that's all they should be doing. No deep feature work, they really should be focusing on alerts, what's causing them, how to minimize, triaging and then retro-ing so they're always being pared down.\n\nMake sure you are keeping an eye on workload too. You may need to think about increasing the number of people on your team if actual feature work isn't getting done because you're busy fighting fires."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:9",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40937269",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by KaiserPro on dashboards at a large financial news site, on 'Binance built a 100PB log service with Quickwit'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. Describes a large financial news organisation, which is why it is read into seg:enterprise.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2024-07-11",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I was at a large financial news site, They were a total splunk shop. We had lots real steel machines shipping and chunking _loads_ of logs. Every team had a large screen showing off key metrics. Most of the time they were badly maintained and broken, so only the _really_ key metrics worked. Great for finding out what went wrong, terrible at alerting when it went wrong.\n\nHowever, over the space of about three years we shifted organically over to graphite+grafana. There wasn't a top down push, but once people realised how easy it was to make a dashboard, do templating and generally keep things working, they moved in droves. It also helped that people put metrics emitting system into the underlying hosting app library.\n\nWhat really sealed the deal was the non-tech business owners making or updating dashboards. They managed to take pure tech metrics and turn them into service/business metrics."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:10",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36495756",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by hinkley on telemetry coverage and unused dashboards, on 'Monitoring Is a Pain'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2023-06-27",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "My company does a lot of things the dumbest way possible but they've always been good at tracing service requests and telemetry. Any time correlation IDs were missing was considered a regression. And we are quick to agree to action items that increase visibility from a postmortem. The one complaint I have is we don't move things out of Splunk into graphs as fast as I'm comfortable with.\n\nBut the thing I see we are all missing for telemetry is a sense of coverage. We changed backends last year and as we were wrapping that up I found big chunks of code that were missing coverage. Meanwhile we found a bunch of stats that nobody looks at, meaning the SNR goes down over time. There are only so many dashboards I can watch.\n\nSo some sense of code blocks missing telemetry, and data that is collected but has no queries, queries that exist but never run (that dashboard nobody remembers making and isn't in the runbooks anywhere) would be a great goal for a next level of maturity."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:11",
      "url": "https://henrikgerdes.me/blog/2025-11-grafana-mess/",
      "title": "I can't recommend Grafana anymore",
      "venue": "Henrik Gerdes' personal blog. Independently owned, not vendor-sponsored; the post reached the Hacker News front page (245 points, 124 comments) as item 45934940, where a Grafana Labs employee replied (see src:21). Note for the next run: that HN thread is heavily seeded by competing vendors (VictoriaMetrics and OpenObserve staff, see src:22), so the thread itself is not buyer voice even though this post is.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-11-14",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Grafana OnCall is deprecated, and Grafana Agent and Agent Flow were deprecated within 2-3 years. They also deprecated Angular within Grafana and switched to React for dashboards. This broke most existing dashboards. Grafana just released Mimir 3.0 ... now they use a message broker. Yes, Mimir in version 3.0 needs Apache Kafka to work. it renders to 6k lines in the default state. I just don't know what will change next. I want stability for my monitoring; I want it boring. I can finally stop explaining to my boss why we need to re-structure the monitoring stack every year. all this together makes me uncomfortable recommending Grafana stuff. monitoring is a necessity - not the product ... I do not want to revisit the monitoring setup every x weeks."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:12",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=44326664",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by vbezhenar on assembling monitoring for Kubernetes, on 'What would a Kubernetes 2.0 look like'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. Excerpt of a longer numbered list; the quoted item is point 4.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2025-06-20",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "I have some experience with Kubernetes, both managed and self-maintained.\n\n4. Implement built-in monitoring. Built-in centralized logs. Built-in UI. Right now, kubeadm cluster if not usable. You need to spend a lot of time, intalling prometheus, loki, grafana, configure dashboards, configure every piece of software. Those are very different softwares from different vendors. It's a mess. It requires a lot of processing power and RAM to work. It should not be like that.\n\nSo basically I want something like Linux. Which just works. I don't need to set up Prometheus to look at my 15-min load average or CPU consumption. I don't need to set up Loki to look at logs, I have journald which is good enough for most tasks."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:13",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=30183011",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by chosenken asking for a split on-prem/cloud offering, on 'Grafana Incident: Smart incident management for your teams'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. Posted on a Grafana Labs product announcement thread, but the commenter is a user of the stack, not the vendor.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2022-02-02",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Would it be possible to have a split offering, with both on prem and cloud? In my mind I would prefer to have things like Prometheus, Logs, and Metrics stored on prem mainly due to the volume of logs and metrics we create. Then use Grafana cloud for Grafana Dashboards, Loki logs, and incident management that pull directly from my on prem data stores. I bring this up as it may be cost prohibitive for us to store our metrics in the cloud ( we make so many metrics and logs! ) but I would love to off load hosting the front end. Grafana cloud takes care of managing and maintaining Grafana Dashboard and backend database, Authentication, updates, ect. I'm fine hosting Prometheus and Loki locally, have been for a long time! I just get annoyed having to host Grafana and setting it up, the database up, configuring auth, etc."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:14",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=12372423",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by user5994461 on the limits of the open-source monitoring stack, on 'Comparison of opensource time series db's'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. Excerpt. Dated 2016, which is why pain:free-stack-hits-a-wall is marked low confidence.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2016-08-27",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "The standard proven open-source stack:\n\ncollectd/statd (metrics collection + whipser/graphite (storage) + grafana (cute graphs and dashboards).\n\nThe latest fad is to replace graphite with prometheus (which is better in some aspects but has it own fault).\n\nBoth these open source tools will satisfy your purpose.\n\nHARDCORE LIMITATIONS: Both these open source tools are entirely single node. There is no form of sharding nor high availability nor horizontal scaling.\n\n(Rules of thumb: Should be fine up to 100 hosts and applications. Then get ready to throw big hardware and tune retention aggressively.)\n\nIt's pretty clear that the open source solutions don't scale and are hard to maintain... so what's next when we outgrow them?"
    },
    {
      "id": "src:15",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=24128852",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by halfmatthalfcat on Prometheus cardinality limits, on 'Systems Monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/24128852).",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2020-08-12",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "You get high enough (hitting a metric with >100k unique labels), queries become unmanageable and incredibly slow when backed by the stock datastore (tsdb). However there are backing datastores (TimescaleDB, InfluxDB, VictoriaMetrics, etc) that ingest Prometheus metrics and enable higher cardinality."
    },
    {
      "id": "src:16",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36923243",
      "title": "Hacker News comment by jrockway on high-cardinality metrics and logs",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API (https://hn.algolia.com/api/v1/items/36923243). Excerpt of a longer comment.",
      "speaker": "practitioner",
      "published": "2023-07-29",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "Post-processing into an efficient retrieval system is the key. You can tail the logs and send span starts/ends to Jaeger, and you can tail the logs and send summarized metrics to Prometheus or InfluxDB. I really like having the too-high-cardinality raw data around, though, so I can analyze specific failing requests. For example, you wouldn't want to have per-request \"tx_bytes\" metrics in Prometheus; the cardinality is too high and it blows up the whole system. But if you log your metrics, then you find the request (grep for lines that contain the x-request-id), and you can see exact timestamps for when every megabyte of data was sent along with everything else that happened in between.\n\nLogs end up being a lot of data, but storing a terabyte of logs to save me a week of debugging is the biggest no-brainer in software engineering."
    },
