    {
      "id": "src:21",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45963212",
      "title": "Hacker News reply by buro9, a Grafana Labs employee, to 'I can't recommend Grafana anymore'",
      "venue": "Hacker News. Comment text read via the HN Algolia API. The commenter discloses he works at Grafana Labs.",
      "speaker": "seller",
      "published": "2025-11-18",
      "pulled": "2026-09-17",
      "verbatim": "A reply of sorts, and a standard disclaimer that I work at Grafana Labs. > They also deprecated Angular within Grafana and switched to React for dashboards. This broke most existing dashboards. we did, I think the entire journey was 7 years long, communicated many times, over at least 6 major releases. maintaining dashboards in two languages increased complexity, whilst reducing compatibility, and gave a very large security surface to be worried about. we communicated clearly, provided migration tools, put it in release notes, updated docs, repeated it at conferences and on community calls. arguably we went too slow, and should've ripped the band-aid off. what I would say is that for most people the boring and slow path does still exist, and it's still good... just use Prometheus, a logging option of your choice, and simple Grafana dashboards and alerts.",
      "unused": "Kept but cited nowhere. It is the company's own answer to pain:stack-never-stays-still and confirms the migrations happened, but a seller cannot support a pain statement or a quote. It is here so the positioning step has the company's existing public response to this pain, and so a re-run does not treat it as buyer voice."
    },
    {
